Fastest tested row counts

1024 × 1024 favored 4 rows at 85.445 ms and 1.25× the one-row throughput.
2048 × 2048 favored 4 rows at 680.471 ms and 1.24× the one-row throughput.
4096 × 4096 favored 4 rows at 5271.569 ms and 1.27× the one-row throughput.

Compiler remarks and optimized IR from the benchmark build showed
four-wide vector operations in the full-group column loops for 2, 4,
and 8 rows. Clang could not prove it was safe to reorder memory
operations in the 16-row loop, which used scalar output updates.
The changing working set and the change in generated code both
affect this comparison.

At 4096 columns, 4 active rows span 64 KiB of C and 8 span 128 KiB.
The 8-row result took 42.1% longer than reordered at that size.
